eating this matzo ball soup makes me  feel warm and fuzzy inside it's the  perfect food for a cold day hi I'm Mike  today I'll show you how to make matzo  ball soup matzo ball soup is made from  matzo meal which is used to make the  unleavened flatbread that we eat for  Passover the Torah explains that because  the Jews left Egypt quickly there wasn't  time for their bread to rise and  therefore matzah serves as a reminder of  their escape this recipe is special to  me because it came from my grandparents  my grandparents were very proud of the  fact that their matzo balls were light  and fluffy  rather than dense there's a bit of  controversy surrounding whether or not  it's okay to use baking powder in matzo  balls for my grandparents it was the  secret ingredient for keeping the balls  light and fluffy some people feel like  it doesn't match the spirit of Passover  because it's technically a leavening  agent however its mineral based not  grain based and so it's not considered  part of the food group known as comets  it's prohibited on Passover they're also  a bunch of baking powder brands that are  kosher for Passover if you'd prefer to  not use baking powder you can absolutely  substitute it with seltzer  there are countless ways to make matzo  ball soup and no one way is the right  way this is just what I remember from my  own childhood I saw my grandparents  every weekend of my childhood and my  grandma was like a second mom to me I  had your average American Ashkenazi  Jewish upbringing my mom is Jewish and  she took my sister and me to Temple when  we were growing up I had a Bar Mitzvah  and these days I can phonetically read  Hebrew but I truly don't know what most  of the words mean anymore I like growing  up in a Jewish household a lot it was a  little odd being the only practicing  Jewish kid in my high school class but  as time has passed I've gained more of  an appreciation for Judaism I really  love being Jewish it's given me a really  beautiful community over the years  freezing matzo balls doesn't affect  their taste or texture at all especially  if you're prepping a large number of  matzo balls  you can absolutely freeze them ahead of  time my grandparents chose to freeze  them before putting them into the soup  at the end to make sure they would  retain their dumpling shape in a warm  liquid they didn't want to risk the  matzo balls disintegrating into a matzah  stew when I was a kid for Passover we  would go over to my grandparents house  and they would host a big seder with  family and friends nowadays one of my  friends in Los Angeles  usually hosts the Seder at their house  and out of the kindness of their heart  they invited me over to it I loved a lot  of the Jewish holidays but Passover  might actually be my favorite one just  it's tied to so many wonderful family  memories my favorite part of it was  singing the four questions with my  sister traditionally it's only the  youngest person at the table who recites  the four questions but my sister and I  would share the duty because we like to  do it together  the Seder Plate and everything that goes  on it looks lovely there's a few foods  for Passover that maybe are harder to  capture on camera matzo ball soup might  be a little gray odd-looking but it's so  delicious  appearances can be deceiving but maybe  you'll look at this and you'll think  it's absolutely delicious whether or not  you're Jewish if you're going to a  Passover Seder I hope you have a  wonderful time and enjoy your matzo ball  soup oh yes  [Music]
 

